11. Methods

11.1 Data Preparation

Astrophysical datasets from Gaia DR3, JWST MAST, and SDSS were preprocessed using a unified ingestion pipeline. Phase‑space coordinates were transformed into a common reference frame, and baryonic/dark‑matter particle states were initialized using catalog‑derived mass, luminosity, and spectral parameters. Voronoi tessellation was applied to the spatial domain to generate density‑adaptive cells for local dynamical updates.

11.2 Manifold Initialization

The baryonic graph was constructed by linking stellar and gas‑cloud nodes using weighted adjacency matrices derived from local density and velocity dispersion. Dark‑matter halo fields were initialized using analytic NFW‑like profiles and catalog‑derived halo masses. The combined manifold was stored in LDCKEG encrypted space.

11.3 Adhesion Dynamics

Adhesion forces were computed using the ISM surface‑tension formulation:

[ F_{\text{adhesion}}(r_i) = \sum_j r_{ij} A_{ij} \frac{r_{ij}}{|r_{ij}|} \left( 1 - \exp\left(-\frac{|r_{ij}|}{l_0}\right) \right). ]

This method emphasizes short‑range baryonic clustering and filament formation. Adhesion updates were applied homomorphically to encrypted particle states.

11.4 Cohesion Dynamics

Cohesion forces were computed using gravitational and MHD kernels:

[ F_{\text{cohesion}}(r_i) = G m_i \sum_j \frac{m_j r_{ji}}{r_{ji}^3}

\text{kernel}(W(r,h)). ]

The gravitational term governs large‑scale collapse, while the MHD kernel governs flux‑conservative evolution in magnetized regions.

11.5 Cryptographic Invariant Computation

At each timestep, the following invariants were computed:

inertia tensor (I)

total angular momentum (L_{\text{total}})

Euler characteristic (\chi_I)

structural traces and determinants

These invariants were hashed into the dynamic cipher state:

[ K_{dyn} = H512(\text{Tr}(I) \parallel \det(I) \parallel L_{\text{total}} \parallel \chi_I). ]

The cipher state governed homomorphic update permissions and manifold integrity.

11.6 GPU Execution

Encrypted particle updates were executed on CUDA and SYCL backends. All arithmetic, memory access, and solver operations were performed on encrypted data. Multi‑GPU parallelism was used for large‑scale manifold evolution.

12. Results

12.1 Filament Formation

Simulations demonstrated the emergence of baryonic filaments driven by adhesion dynamics. Filament thickness correlated with local ISM density and adhesion coefficient (A_{ij}). Filaments remained stable under cohesion‑driven gravitational collapse.

12.2 Halo–Baryon Coupling

Dark‑matter halo potentials induced large‑scale baryonic inflow. Cohesion dynamics reinforced filament alignment with halo gravitational gradients. The resulting structures resembled observed cosmic‑web morphologies.

12.3 MHD‑Driven Structural Evolution

Regions with strong magnetic fields exhibited flux‑conservative evolution. MHD kernels produced anisotropic cohesion forces, generating sheet‑like structures and suppressing collapse along field lines.

12.4 Tessellation‑Driven Manifold Shifts

Voronoi tessellation enabled adaptive resolution. High‑density regions exhibited smaller cells and more frequent updates, improving accuracy in collapse zones. Low‑density regions evolved smoothly with larger cells.

12.5 Invariant Stability

Across all simulations, cryptographic invariants remained stable. No unauthorized manifold mutations were detected. Homomorphic updates preserved invariant consistency across GPU execution.

